s your  mounting medium for fluorescent samples  you want to make sure that there's a an  anti fade present an anti fade mounting  medium will slow the photo bleaching and  I'll talk about that again in the next  slide if you want to just visualize your  sample under the microscope and then  throw away your slide you want a non  curing or liquidy mounting medium that  does not harden such as our slow fade  diamond anti-fade mountains if you want  it to if you want to archive your slides  for imaging later days two weeks later  or even months you want a hardening or  curing mountain for fluorescent samples  I recommend our prolonged diamond ante  fade mountains or prolonged glass for  chroma genex slides you'll often go with  cytosine sixty or histone mount  for instance our DPX which hardened very  hard you have to chip it off if you  wanted to remove it  whatever mountain you choose make sure  it has a very good refractive index  close to glass like the glass used in  your cover slip and your objective lens  usually around 1.5 to for the best  resolution this will ensure that you  have good resolution especially at the  high magnification objectives if you're  doing fluorescence make sure your  mountain does not itself Auto  fluorescent to give background  photo bleaching so if you have a  fluorescent sample when you shine light  on it using your microscope you'll  notice that the dye starts to go dim  over time as you're looking at it that's  photo bleaching it's a it's a photo  physical effect on the structure of the  dye causing destruction of the dye  structure and so it stops fluorescing  you can use anti fade mounting mediums  like prolong gold and prolong diamond  prolong glass these have components in  it that are anti oxidants and free  radical scavengers that can vastly slow  this photo bleaching effect I highly  recommend you use an anti fade mounting  medium for any fluorescent detection we  have a web resource down there at thermo  Fisher comm slash anti fades to help  with that  also we've recently reintroduced  prolonged blasts so if you have  especially if you have very thick  samples the refractive index is very  critical in order to image deep into the  tissue so with prolonged glass we've  demonstrated you can image 70 or even a  hundred micron thick samples and still  detect the signal down deep inside with  very good resolution compared for  instance to a competitor of ours here  where you can't see so far a huge  improvement in depth of detection hole  mounts very thick samples and spheroids  for instance are becoming much more  common now so this is becoming a more  important product here for instance you  can see some images of spheroids labeled  with a couple of our dyes cell rocks  deep red might attract or orange and  Hearst for nuclei that have been imaged  as deep as 100 microns with our  prolonged glass ok let's talk about the  instrument platforms typically you'll  want to use a microscope there's a lot  of different kinds out there the most  common is a wide field microscope either  inverted like our FL auto and FL auto 2  that you see here on our evos product  line but there's a lot of different  microscope companies out there wide  field means that you don't have any  specialized techniques to limit just  your plane of view but there's also  confocal microscopes which use optical  properties to image only a thin section  through your tissue at a time to cut out  extraneous light above and below where  you're wanting to image  there's also multiphoton systems and  light sheet microscopy systems so  there's a lot of different systems out  there but whatever system you use there  are some important hardware concerns  always make sure everything is clean you  know  use clean slides and clean cover slips  without debris on them make sure your  objective lens lenses are cleaned  regularly and professor  generally and choose your objective  lenses wisely with the right objective  magnification numerical aperture align  your light source so with us with our  Evo systems we use an LED light cube  which has the light source built into it  so there's nothing to align which is  marvelous but a lot of typical systems  out there will use a mercury bulb for  instance that has to be carefully  aligned so it's in the centre of your  view and adjusted so that's uniform in  intensity our systems don't need that  also the condensers if you're doing  transmitted light you have to make sure  that your condenser is aligned so it  gives nice centered and uniform  illumination again our Evo systems  there's no alignment necessary for that  also make sure that you have the right  filter sets or like cubes for your diet  I mentioned the spectra viewer earlier  so here you can see an example in the  middle of typical like cubes used in  typical imaging microscopes for  fluorescent imaging re vo's microscopes  use special light cubes that you see on  the right which has everything built  into it including the light source  you  on the left here you can see an example  of an image where the light source was  not carefully uniform and centered so  you see it's brighter on the left and on  the right in the middle is an example of  an objective lens that has not been  cleaned well you can see little specks  in the center there of debris that's on  the lens it's quite common in  microscopes that if people are doing hi  Maggie mijung that they accidentally get  immersion oil on non immersion oil  lenses which can be difficult to clean  so talk to your image facilitator to  make sure that it's cleaned correctly if  you accidentally drag an immersion oil  in a non immersion oil lens also in this  image you can see the objective is  butting up against the stage which can  damage the objective so you have to look  for damage to be very careful by the way  here's a good link to our Evo's  microscope systems just contact us if  you're interested in those systems  remember proper Hardware gives you  efficient detection what's the point in  going through all that protocol to label  if you're not going to have the best  hardware to detect it alright once  you've taken your image well when you  when you have your microscope remember  that you're imaging you have to have  special imaging software to capture it  with your camera system and you have to  optimize those image settings very  carefully namely the exposure time that  you're detecting the gain setting on  your camera the light intensity if  that's adjustable as it is with our Evo  system with confocal microscopes there's  a number of other factors you have to  take into consideration like your laser  intensity the rastering back and forth  on your sample the pinhole size and just  taking the image isn't enough you also  have to think about how you're saving  your image if you if you have a JPEG  format for instance remember it  compresses the image but it also  preserves and 8-bits the color as well  as maybe a scale bar or other  annotations but if you're doing  quantitative  analysis where you're comparing the  intensity between images you want to  save as a 16-bit usually 16-bit TIFF  image which is black and white doesn't  save the color or scale bars but this is  important because that that gives you  the full dynamic range of the camera at  16 bits so you can more carefully  compare the quantitative relative  fluorescence between the images here's  an example screenshot from our Evo's FL  auto 2 imaging system and the software  used and you can see some little bars on  that lower right that you can adjust the  light intensity exposure and gain  settings as well as the focus this image  here is of a kidney section labeled with  Alexa fluor 488 and it's pretty dim so I  would say this is too dim you can adjust  the lighting but now the light and  exposure time are too high  you can see you're saturating the signal  matter-of-fact the software will color  the saturated pixels blue so you want to  back off on that exposure time to  something like this this is an optimized  image so there's little to no saturated  pixels or it's sub saturation this is  where you want to be as well as good  focus here's an example of some cells  that I took which are not a good image  you can see that the cells are not  centered in the image the green  wavelength you can't really pick out  very well because it's too low of an  exposure time or gain setting the red is  a Texas red for mitochondria and it's  too bright too long of an exposure time  saturating the pixels and there's a bit  of blue background maybe it's a little  hard for you to see here whereas if I  optimize all that now you can see a much  better section or image here where the  actin is nice and bright and the green  the mitochondria are not too bright and  I've adjusted the gain so that the Dappy  signal and the nuclei is not too much in  the background  remember optimize settings gives you  accurate data  once you've taken your images now you  want to go to image analysis software  not acquisition software but analysis  software image J for instance is a  freeware out there but it can be a  little bit difficult to use it's not  very user friendly we offer here at  Thermo Fisher Scientific the Celeste  imaging software which is combated  compatible with our evos line of  microscopes and you can see here a three  color image where each of the three  colors is in black and white blue green  and red and I've merged them in the  lower right you can also adjust here you  can see in the red box the contrast and  gamma and brightness of the image and  with image analysis software now you can  analyze your images the most typical way  to analyze to compare intensities  between sections or sorry images is to  use a region of interest here you can  see in yellow some regions that I've  drawn on the cells for this tubulin  labeling sorry actin labeling and a  circle in the background so that I can  subtract the background fluorescence  from the specific fluorescence another  way to detect or measure your cells is  with thresholded a certain range of  intensities that i want to measure but  this is too low you can see there are  areas that are not selected here that's  much better  although maybe it's over selected a bit  and here it's way too high so I've over  thresholded now with our celeste imaging  software has the smart segmentation  where you can actually train it over the  course of just a few seconds to a minute  to detect only the regions of interest  and subtract out the regions of  non-interest so you can see this is very  nice  actin detection with our smart  segmentation whatever you do segmenting  yourself actually let me go back  the Celeste software has a  lot of different capabilities beyond  just measuring intensities you can even  do like 3d visualization or you can  count cells with it or do a parent/child  analysis or look at cell migration over  time there's a lot of different uses  that I'm not going to go into all the  functionalities of Celeste software here  okay and then finally I want to just say  a few words about imaging ethics and  this is something that's not touched  upon enough out there what can you and  can't you do to your images first off  never add anything to your image except  maybe annotations you want your data to  be very honest if you do do any  post-acquisition manipulations there are  some very important points to keep in  mind okay so don't add anything to your  image except annotations don't  selectively enhance one area without  performing the same manipulation on the  whole image don't compare two images  that have not undergone the same  post-acquisition manipulation so you  don't want to brighten one image and dim  another and compare them because that  would just be false data and don't crop  your images in any way that would remove  valid relevant data and if you do crop  it just make sure to you know do that to  other comparable images as well for  instance if you're focusing only on one  area whatever changes you make make sure  that it's reflected in the text of your  paper or even in the captions of the  image always remember ethical imaging  means trustworthy data and replicable  data okay and then finally these last  two slides are just some great links  that you can go to if you want to come  back to this presentation later we have  a number of different  immunohistochemistry and histology and  immuno Saito chemistry pages and  articles on our webpage and here are  many of them that third one there is the  this webinar  specific to this webinar the five steps  to publication quality images and then  finally there's some other useful web  resources I mentioned the spectra viewer  for instance but we also have floor for  selection guide our molecular probes  school of fluorescence that talks about  a lot of the basics of imaging that's a  really great page I highly recommend  that anyone starting out or undergrads  or even if you just want some refreshers  go to the school of fluorescence to get  some great tips  okay so I'll open up for any questions  that you may have  thank you Jason for your informative  presentation we will now start the live  Q&amp;A portion of the webinar if you have  any questions you'd like to ask please  do so now just click on the answer a  question box located on the far left of  your screen and we'll answer as many  questions as we have time for okay let's  get started  Jason our first question is what is the  difference between immunofluorescence  immunohistochemistry and in your nose  phytochemistry are these terms  interchangeable well sometimes people  use one for the other but technically  speaking immunohistochemistry deals with  immuno labeling of tissues immuno Saito  chemistry deals with immuno labeling of  cells and culture and both of which fall  under the general umbrella of  immunofluorescence sometimes you'll see  them abbreviated as I F or I HC or ICC  and used interchangeably even on our  webpages but often one that's good for  immunohistochemistry is also good for  immuno Saito chemistry  Sookie Jason our next question now it  seems to be a lot of different  permeabilization detergent in immuno  protocols triton x-100 between 20  saponin and p 40 and a couple others now  how do i choose between them  that's a good question the most common  detergents that are used for immuno  protocols are non ionic detergents  especially triton x-100 but also tween  20 NP 40 or non idaite p 40 as it's also  known as also used sometimes these are  pretty strong detergents that poke holes  in the membranes but they're strong  enough that they can also extract  proteins associated with the membranes  for most immuno Saito chemistry and  histochem astray those are the most  typically used because you really want  to make some big holes there for these  large antibodies to enter but this can  be a problem especially for those  membrane bound proteins if you're trying  to detect for instance surface antigens  on cells and in those cases you might  want to use a gentler detergent saponin  for instance which is named from support  a plant it's a plant derived detergent  actually interacts with membrane  cholesterol selectively removing them  and leaving smaller holes in the  membrane often saponin is used for flow  cytometry and then there's some others  like digit onin for instance that are  even gentler but generally speaking  Triton is probably the most popular  Thank You Jason and that brings me to  this next question that we've received  several from several attendees now what  do you recommend for cleaning objectives  detergent ethanol  when you want when you discover that  there's some debris or something on your  objectives first you want to sort of  spritz it off with a little air can if  it's actual debris that's stuck on the  lens such as dirt of some sort then the  next step is to use a gentle detergent  there's a common one called sparkle out  there it's pink colored but each of the  microscope companies also sell  detergents and what you'll do is you'll  use a lint-free lens paper and a  cotton-tipped applicator you'll put a  drop of that detergent on and use the  lens paper and the cotton tipped  applicator on top of the lens paper to  roll that paper across and absorb off  the detergent with just a little bit of  force from the cotton-tipped applicator  and some slice circular motion and this  will remove most of the debris that may  be on there still in some cases though  that debris doesn't come off with a  gentle detergent immersion oil for  instance is very difficult to get off in  those cases you can go with ethanol  which is a gentler solvent or  isopropanol to put a drop on there and  again remove with clean lens paper you  don't want to use really strong solvents  like for instance chloroform or acetone  unless you absolutely positively have to  because those stronger solvents can  degrade the glue around the lenses  thank you Jason in the interest of time  this will have to be our last question  but I want to remind our audience that  any questions submitted during the  presentation that were not answered out  loud Jason will be answering them via  email following the presentation now  Jason we have a viewer inmate their city  mate after they mounted their slide they  found that there were bubbles under the  coverslip  what can they do or what can he do to  get rid of them that's a good question  bubbles can come from several sources  they can come when you're actually  dropping the mounting solution down on  to your sample  sometimes bubbles will come out of your  dropper other times there can be air  trapped inside the tissue and when the  mountains cures or hardens it'll  compress the tissue forcing the air out  of that tissue usually if it's from the  tissue it's very tiny bubbles that  appear only over the tissue whereas if  it's air droplets that come out during  the deposition of the mountain they're  usually larger and off and off off the  tissue section if you're using an  aqueous based Mountain like our  prolonged products you can remove the  cover slip by putting the slide into  warm PBS solution maybe at 37 degrees  with a little bit of movement and it  slowly dissolves away the prolong and  the cover slip will slide off and at  that point you've lost the air bubbles  and you can remount the sample with some  new prolong if you're mounting us in a  solvent based mountain though you have  to actually use solvents to remove the  mounting medium and it's not always  successful and can cause damage to your  tissue also that's another reason to use  an aqueous based mountain  thank you again Jason now before we go  I'd like to thank our audience for  joining us today and for their  interesting questions and a quick  reminder that the questions we did not  have time for today and those submitted  during the on demand period will be  addressed by the speaker via the contact  information you provided at the time of  registration I would also like to thank  Bob Rose and our sponsor Thermo Fisher  Scientific for underwriting today's  educational webcast this webcast can be  viewed on-demand through October 2018  Bob routes will alert you via email when  this is available for replay we  encourage you to share that email with  your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event until next time  thanks for joining us goodbye
 

